name: follow the mount
after: 22-disks
intro: /proc/mounts has one line per mounted filesystem: device, mount point,
intro: type, then a comma-separated option list. It is the ground truth for how
intro: this machine is actually assembled -- and where you prove root is locked.
---
setup: Field 2 of /proc/mounts is the mount point, field 3 the filesystem type.
q: print the filesystem type of the root mount in /proc/mounts, and nothing else
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $3}' /proc/mounts
ref: awk
cmp: ref
hint: match the line whose field 2 is exactly "/"
---
setup: The fourth field of /proc/mounts is the comma-separated option list.
q: print the mount options of the root filesystem from /proc/mounts
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $4}' /proc/mounts
ref: awk
cmp: ref
hint: same match as before, one field further right
---
setup: noexec on a mount means nothing there can be executed -- a defence you
setup: want to confirm, not assume.
q: list every mount point in /proc/mounts that is mounted noexec
a: awk '$4 ~ /noexec/ {print $2}' /proc/mounts
a: grep noexec /proc/mounts | awk '{print $2}'
ref: awk
cmp: ref
hint: search field 4 for noexec, print field 2 when it is there
